\begin{theorem}[Inverse Function Theorem for Manifolds]
\label{thm:inverse-function-theorem-manifolds}

\uses{lem:local-diffeomorphism-from-invertible-differential}

\dcref{chA:A.14}



Suppose $M$ and $N$ are smooth manifolds and $F: M \to N$ is a smooth map. If the linear map $dF_p$ is invertible at some point $p \in M$, then there exist connected neighborhoods $U_0$ of $p$ and $V_0$ of $F(p)$ such that $F|_{U_0}: U_0 \to V_0$ is a diffeomorphism.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}

\uses{lem:local-diffeomorphism-from-invertible-differential}
By Lemma \ref{lem:local-diffeomorphism-from-invertible-differential} there are open sets $U \ni p$ and $V \ni F(p)$ such that $F|_U : U \to V$ is a diffeomorphism. Let $U_0$ be the connected component of $p$ in $U$. A smooth manifold is locally connected, being locally homeomorphic to a normed space, in which the balls are convex and hence connected; therefore $U_0$ is open, and by construction it is connected and contains $p$. Put $V_0 = F(U_0)$. Then $V_0$ is open, being the image of an open subset of $U$ under the diffeomorphism $F|_U$, it is connected as the continuous image of a connected set, and it contains $F(p)$. Finally $F|_{U_0} : U_0 \to V_0$ is a diffeomorphism, being the restriction of the diffeomorphism $F|_U$ to an open subset of its domain and to the image of that subset.
\end{proof}

The proof of the manifold statement rests on the corresponding statement for the coordinate representation, together with the fact that invertibility of a bounded operator is an open condition.

